import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { REPO } from '../../lib/site';
---
<Layout
  title="PHP2Shell, explained plainly"
  description="What the PHP2Shell upload-flood class of attack is, why only a PHP extension can stop it, and exactly what Howdah does and does not verify — described by shape only, with no exploit."
  path="/blog/php2shell-explained/"
>
  <article class="post">
    <div class="pagehead">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blog/">Blog</a> / <span aria-current="page">PHP2Shell explained</span></p>
        <h1>PHP2Shell, explained plainly</h1>
        <p class="lede"><time datetime="2026-10-23">23 October 2026</time></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>On 21 October 2026 the PHP project patched two linked flaws, CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619, which security researchers named PHP2Shell. This is a plain-language explainer of the <em>class</em> of problem and where Howdah fits. It describes the attack only by its shape — how many parts and how large — and contains no working exploit.</p>

      <h2>What happens</h2>
      <p>When a browser uploads a form, it sends a <code>multipart/form-data</code> request: a body split into many &ldquo;parts&rdquo;, each with a small header and some content. PHP parses that body and builds <code>$_POST</code> and <code>$_FILES</code> before any of your application code runs.</p>
      <p>As publicly reported, a crafted multipart request makes PHP use a lot of memory while it reads the <em>headers</em> of upload parts. A body well under PHP's default 8&nbsp;MB <code>post_max_size</code> can still hold hundreds or thousands of tiny, nearly-empty parts. According to the reports, corrupted state from that can lead to remote code execution without the attacker logging in. According to the same reports, the official fix ships for PHP 8.2 and newer, and older branches get no official patch. PHP 8.1 and earlier still run a very large share of the web.</p>

      <h2>Why only a PHP extension can stop it</h2>
      <p>The damage happens <em>during PHP's own parsing</em>, before your code runs. That rules out the usual places you might add a check:</p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>A firewall written in PHP (for example via <code>auto_prepend_file</code>) runs after the upload has already been parsed into <code>$_POST</code>/<code>$_FILES</code> — too late.</li>
        <li><code>opcache.preload</code> runs once at server start, never per request.</li>
        <li>A network firewall sees bytes on the wire, not how PHP will interpret them, so it cannot cheaply tell a giant legitimate upload from a part-flood.</li>
        <li>A compiled extension runs <em>inside</em> PHP's upload parser, so it can count parts as PHP reads them and stop the request early. That is what Howdah does.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Howdah does — and what we verified</h2>
      <p>Howdah enforces limits on the number of parts and files, the size of names, and a memory budget, counting parts while PHP parses the body, so over-limit parts are never allocated, and refusing the request before your script runs. Those critical limits block by default, even in detect mode.</p>
      <div class="note">
        <h3>What we did and did not verify</h3>
        <p>Our tests reproduce only the harmless <strong>structure</strong> of such a flood: how many parts and fields, and how large. We did <strong>not</strong> reproduce the CVE's <code>session.upload_progress</code> header-parsing path, so we do <strong>not</strong> claim Howdah blocks CVE-2026-109618 on a stock configuration.</p>
        <p>What we measured: PHP's own <code>max_input_vars</code> and <code>max_file_uploads</code> already keep the flood shape we built small on a default box. On a box where an admin has raised those caps, the same shape used 127 to 173&nbsp;MB against a 128&nbsp;MB limit, and Howdah's default limits refuse it long before that. Howdah's value is strongest on raised-cap servers and on old PHP with no official fix.</p>
      </div>
      <p>The incident details above come from public reports, which we are checking against the official advisories (GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w, GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp, and php.net); we will correct this page if they differ. You can see Howdah's verdict on a defanged, shape-only version of this flood in the <a href="/demo/">browser demo</a>, and exactly which PHP versions we have tested on the <a href="/coverage/">coverage page</a>.</p>

      <p>Howdah is open source; the core, the tests and the measurements are on <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

